    "difficulty": "Development",
    "difficultyReason": "Parts a and b are heavily scaffolded: the boxes prompt adding the indices, equating them and solving a one-step equation. Part c, the hardest part, has no scaffold. The student must combine 6^{2x}×6^x into 6^{3x}, equate indices and solve 3x=15. This is a single product-of-powers step followed by one-step solving, so it is a little above simple index simplification (index-t1-q9). It is below index-t1-q14, which has no scaffolding and includes 49 rewritten as 7^2, three-term products and unknowns on both sides. It is roughly level with index-t1-q12, so it sits nearer the lower reference.",
